Title:
Moonwalk++ (Moonwalk--): PoC Combining Stack Moonwalking and Memory Self-Encryption

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
Moonwalk++ is a proof-of-concept Windows injection loader that combines “Stack Moonwalking” (call stack spoofing / caller removal) with in-memory self-encryption of executing shellcode. It uses ROP chains to desynchronize stack unwinding from real control flow while encrypting/decrypting the payload to reduce in-memory inspection visibility. The PoC is intentionally rough and primarily exists to validate claims from the accompanying blog post (“Malware Just Got Its Free Passes Back!”). It is hard-coded to target OneDrive.exe and relies on locating specific gadgets (notably in wininet.dll and others), making it version/build dependent. It’s mainly useful for red teamers, offensive security researchers, and malware developers studying modern evasion tradecraft and its fragility across environments.

Technical Focus:
- Call stack spoofing / Stack Moonwalking (unwinding desync vs control flow)
- ROP chain construction and gadget hunting across Windows DLLs
- Shellcode self-encryption/decryption during execution
- Remote process injection into OneDrive.exe (PID-based)
- Thread context manipulation (SetThreadContext) and stack pivoting

Use Cases:
- Researching EDR/telemetry blind spots around call stack-based detections
- Prototyping combined stack spoofing + payload encryption execution flows
- Studying gadget reliability issues across Windows versions and DLL builds
- Building defensive detections for anomalous ROP/pivot + context-hijack patterns
- Demonstrating limitations/OPSEC gaps of PoC-grade evasion techniques

Keywords:
Moonwalk++, Stack Moonwalking, call stack spoofing, stack unwinding, ROP, gadget hunting, stack pivot, SetThreadContext, thread hijacking, shellcode encryption, memory self-encryption, OneDrive.exe injection, wininet.dll, KernelBase.dll, Windows API spoofing, EDR evasion, payload concealment, process injection, control-flow desynchronization